// ── da006: THE FULL VOCABULARY, SHAPED RATHER THAN CAPPED ──
// da005 read "nothing too scary or intense" as a CEILING: it deleted CLIMAX
// from the form, lidded drum velocity and capped intensity at 0.62. That was
// the wrong reading and Josh said so plainly: he still wants climaxes, crests
// and every other musical effect built in. He is right. Chill music swells.
// A score that can never peak is not relaxing, it is flat, and flat is its
// own kind of unpleasant.
//
// What actually makes a passage feel intense is not SIZE, it is SUDDENNESS
// and HARSHNESS: a peak that arrives with no warning, lands on a hard
// transient and rides an aggressive rhythm. So the caps are gone and the
// whole dynamic range is back. Instead the peaks are SHAPED:
//   - every section has an arc target, and the score RAMPS between them, so
//     a climax is approached and released instead of switched on;
//   - it rises slower than it falls, which is what a swell actually does;
//   - the peak spends its energy on ARRANGEMENT (wider voicing, the top
//     voice joining, longer pads) as much as on volume.
